<Docs slug="immich">
  <p>
    The LightPhotos desktop app can upload finished photos straight to any
    Immich server you point it at — including one on a box in your closet.
    Open the export form and set <strong>Destination</strong> to
    <strong>Immich</strong>, then paste your server's address into
    <strong>Server URL</strong>, add an <strong>API key</strong>, and click
    <strong>Connect</strong>.
  </p>

  <ul class="lp-article-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Immich is a photo library you host yourself.</strong> It is the
      closest thing to a self-hosted Google Photos, and LightPhotos treats it
      as a first-class export target.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your server address and API key are stored locally.</strong> They
      live in the desktop app's config and are never sent anywhere except your
      own Immich server.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Let the server handle duplicates.</strong> Immich already
      deduplicates by content hash, so re-exporting the same photo is safe.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The web version can't upload to Immich.</strong> Choosing
      <strong>Immich</strong> there shows a note that it only works in the desktop app. Browsers
      block a web page from talking to a server on another site unless that
      server allows it, and Immich doesn't.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Once connected, exporting to Immich works exactly like exporting to disk:
    select your photos, pick an <strong>Album</strong> if you
    want one, and click <strong>Export</strong>. Your adjustments
    are baked in before the upload, so what lands on the server is the
    finished photo.
  </p>
</Docs>
